-- title:
--   Double cones are symmetric under negation
-- statement:
--   Let $E$ be a real inner product space, let $v, h \in E$ and let $\vartheta \in \mathbb{R}$. Writing $D(v,\vartheta)$ for the double cone with axis $v$ and apex angle $\vartheta$ — the union of the cone $\mathcal{C}(v,\vartheta)$ and its reflection $-\mathcal{C}(v,\vartheta)$ — one has
--   $$-h \in D(v,\vartheta) \iff h \in D(v,\vartheta).$$
--
--   Thus a double cone is invariant under the antipodal map, which is precisely the property that distinguishes it from a single cone. Because of it, the relation "$x$ and $y$ are joined through the double cone of axis $v$", i.e. $x - y \in D(v,\vartheta)$, is *symmetric* in the two points; every argument that treats a pair of points without a preferred order relies on this, as does any use of a double cone as an undirected adjacency relation.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The lemma is a `simp` lemma, so negations are normalised away automatically. There are no hypotheses: $v$ need not be a unit vector and $\vartheta$ is an arbitrary real, so the symmetry is built into the definition rather than depending on the geometric regime $0 < \vartheta \le \pi/2$.
-- source:
